{{also|Vig|víg}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Abbreviation of {{m|en|vigorish}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/vɪɡ/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪɡ|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[charge]] taken on [[bet]]s, as by a [[bookie]] or gambling establishment.
#* '''2009''', Wayne L. Winston, ''Mathletics: How Gamblers, Managers, and Sports Enthusiasts Use Mathematics'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=QLI3JJ9CD5QC&pg=PA256&dq=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=kg8GUqfXJcTKkwWNuoFY&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&f=false page 256],
#*: The bookmaker's mean profit per dollar bet is called vigorish or “the vig.” In our example, 11 + 11 = $22 is bet, and the bookmaker wins $1 so the '''vig''' is 1/22 = 4.5%.
# {{lb|en|slang}} Interest from a [[loan shark]]'s loan.
#* '''1973''', [[w:Martin Scorsese|Martin Scorsese]], [[w:Mardik Martin|Mardik Martin]] (screenplay), ''[[w:Mean Streets|Mean Streets]]'', quoted in '''2009''', Ellis Cashmore, ''Martin Scorsese's America'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=mJ-58uKdE7IC&pg=PA118&dq=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=kg8GUqfXJcTKkwWNuoFY&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&f=false page 118],
#*: “You charged a guy from the neighborhood $1800 '''vig'''?” he asks incredulously (“vig” is short for ''vigorish'', meaning a rate of interest from a loan from an illegal moneylender).
#* '''2005''', F. P. Lione, ''The Crossroads'', Midtown Blue Book #2, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=St7_zF_znAcC&pg=PA100&dq=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=XxYGUtuYB4eLkwXFx4DwDA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22vig%22%7C%22vigs%22&f=false page 100],
#*: The guy was probably professional muscle, a leg breaker who collects '''vig''' for a loan shark. (''Vig'' is a mob term for interest on loans to a loan shark.)
# A [[commission]], [[finder's fee]], or similar extra charge.

